  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Where does the water in a tap come from?",
      "a": "It comes along a mains pipe from the water company, already under pressure. The tap does not pump the water; it only opens or closes a gate so the pressure can push water out.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does water come out when I open a tap?",
        "What pushes water out of the tap?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the spindle do?",
      "a": "The spindle is a threaded rod joined to the handle. Turning the handle screws it up or down, which lifts the washer off the seat or presses it back on.",
      "asks": [
        "What is inside a tap?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does my tap drip?",
      "a": "Usually the rubber washer or the seat is worn or dirty, so the washer cannot make a perfect seal. A little water squeezes past all the time, and replacing the washer normally stops it.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you fix a dripping tap?",
        "Why does a tap leak when it is turned off?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the valve seat?",
      "a": "The valve seat is the smooth ring around the hole the water must pass through. When the washer is pressed firmly onto it, no water can get past.",
      "asks": [
        "What does the washer press against?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a quarter-turn tap work?",
      "a": "Many modern taps use two hard ceramic discs with holes in them instead of a washer. A quarter turn of the handle slides the holes into line to let water through, or out of line to stop it.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a ceramic disc tap?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "In a traditional compression tap, a rubber washer pressed onto a valve seat stops the flow.",
    "Ceramic disc taps open and close with a quarter turn, because two discs slide their holes in or out of line."
  ],
